Strata on Gentoo
================

This package installs two things from one upstream source: the inference engine
and, with USE="terminal", the `strata` terminal and API server that start it.

The engine
----------
The engine is built for exactly one GPU backend:

    USE="cuda"   NVIDIA, through the CUDA toolkit
    USE="hip"    AMD, through the ROCm HIP toolchain (one or more amdgpu_targets_*)

The two are mutually exclusive. Without either flag the package does not build:
a CPU-only engine is not something upstream ships.

The engine is compiled for specific NVIDIA compute capabilities, and a binary
without code for your card stops at startup with a message instead of running
slowly. With nothing configured, the build asks the driver which cards this
machine has and builds exact code for them, so a local build needs no setup.

Set the list yourself to build for another machine, or to build one engine for
several cards, in /etc/portage/env/strata and pull it in with
/etc/portage/package.env:

    # /etc/portage/env/strata
    CMAKE_CUDA_ARCHITECTURES="89-real;120-real"

    # /etc/portage/package.env
    sci-ml/strata strata

CUDAARCHS in make.conf is honoured too and serves as the default for the whole
system. The "-real" suffix asks for the card's own machine code; drop it on the
last entry ("89-real;120") to also carry PTX, which the driver can compile for
a card newer than the toolkit knows.

ROCm users select the cards with the amdgpu_targets_* flags. Strata's HIP path
carries a fixed list of wave32 architectures and refuses any other with a
configure error, so the ebuild passes the amdgpu_targets_* selection and never
lets the build probe the host's cards. Validated by upstream on gfx1100 and
gfx1201, reported working on gfx1101 and gfx1200, and building-but-unvalidated
on the rest of the RDNA2/RDNA3/RDNA3.5 list; a target outside that list fails
at configure time. AMD cards need CONFIG_HSA_AMD in the kernel.

The engine is installed as a component under /usr/lib/strata/components, in the
layout upstream's own packaging produces, with a SHA-256 manifest that the
terminal verifies at every start. That is why the package sets RESTRICT="strip":
rewriting the binaries afterwards would break the check.

The terminal
------------
`strata launch` picks a model, downloads it and writes a run config; `strata
serve` runs the OpenAI/Anthropic-compatible API. Both live in the calling user's
~/.local/share/strata, so nothing is written to /usr at run time.

The engine belongs to Portage here. `strata install` and `strata update` refuse
to run and point at emerge instead (files/redact-portage-managed.patch). To
experiment with a per-user engine, export

    STRATA_PACKAGE_MANAGED=0
    STRATA_COMPONENTS_DIR=$HOME/.local/share/strata/components

Pictures
--------
USE="vision" (on by default) builds the picture reader `strata-vision` from
tools/vision, so a prompt can carry images. It is a second CMake project that
builds llama.cpp's mtmd and ggml again, for the same architectures as the
engine: a reader carried over from a different build fails on a card the engine
itself handles.

On CUDA there is one reader, which reads on the GPU when the engine starts it
with --gpu and on the CPU otherwise. On HIP there are two, as upstream's own
packaging ships them: the CPU reader `strata-vision`, which starts even where
the HIP runtime does not load, and `strata-vision-hip` for the card, which the
terminal prefers. Both live in the engine component folder.

With USE="-vision" the package carries no reader and Strata answers text only.

Models
------
Model weights are not packaged and are not downloaded by the ebuild. `strata
launch` fetches what you choose into your own data folder; the upstream model
notes are in the engine's docs directory and at
https://github.com/Niko1221/Strata.

Strata runs one model family, not arbitrary GGUF files. Its catalogue in
data/gguf_fingerprints.json has 17 entries under the qwen, swift, coder,
unsloth and gyro keys, and every one of them is Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (qwen35moe)
in a GSQ-RCO, Q2_0 or UD quantization, split into two shards with the exact
upstream file names. Another model, including a different Qwen3.8 size such as
Qwen3.8-27B (qwen35), is refused by `strata models import`:

    this model type is not supported by the Strata engine (add it as a
    Custom model API with llama-server instead)

For those, a llama.cpp package is the tool - sci-misc/llama-cpp from guru, or
sci-misc/kvmem-llama-cpp and sci-misc/prism-llama-cpp from this overlay: the
engine here is the Flash-Next one and its kernels are written for it. An MTP
drafter file (the -mtp.gguf beside a model) and an mmproj are parts of a
supported selection, not models of their own, and importing either on its own
is refused too.

Let `strata launch` fetch a supported model rather than copying an existing
GGUF into the data folder: it writes the run config the engine needs and
prepares the pack, mtp and mmproj files beside it.
